  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Upper low over the Upper Great Lakes will move \nsoutheast tonight then bounce to the northeast Monday. Showers \nand thunderstorms will move through the region periodically \nthrough Monday night.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are spread across all of the CWA, even\nas far S as the Turnpike, this afternoon. The convection is\nlikely at a peak right now as the forcing from a significant\nshort wave trough is moving through coincident with peak solar \nheating. A lull may be in store as we get under the influence of\nthe NVA behind the trough. But, it will only be brief as a\nstronger wave rounds/warps the base of the upper low over the\nGreat Lakes. That feature is on track to redevelop convection\nover the NW around or just before sunset, and drive more \nSHRA/TSRA through between then and midnight. All of these\nshowers have the potential to make some small hail. A G29KT just\nhappened at CXY as a good SHRA passed, and that shows the \npotential of showers (there was no TS) that may /seem/ a bit too\nfar from the cold pool aloft. \n\nAfter midnight, the coverage of showers should be lower. \nWithout the extra help from the sun and strong forcing, the cold\npool aloft won't be enough to generate more than isolated \nshowers, and thunder will be unlikely overnight.\n\nWe expect not as much coverage to the SHRA/TSRA Monday without\ngood PVA moving across. However, the cold pool aloft and heating\nshould make isolated to scattered convection along the northern\ntier. It is very unlikely that any precip will get south of \nI-80. Some will linger into the night, but it gets dry on\nTuesday with not muggy, but not refreshing dewpoints.",
